🔴 Finding 5: Cleartext SMTP Credentials and Sensitive Information Disclosure
Severity: High (or Critical depending on internal network access)
Affected Component: Notification Tab (Email Information)

Description: The web application exposes highly sensitive email configuration data to low-privileged users. While the UI masks the password visually, the underlying HTML/HTTP response contains the cleartext password for the internal SMTP service account. It also leaks Personally Identifiable Information (PII) and internal infrastructure details.

Observation: While authenticated as the low-privileged Info-User, navigating to Notification -> Email Information exposed the internal SMTP relay hostname (mailhost.fr.world.socgen), a valid employee email address (boubaker-seddik.rebhi@socgen.com), and the service account username (svcsmtpauthfrant). By inspecting the page source/intercepting the request, the cleartext password for this service account was visible (redacted in the attached evidence for security).

Impact: This is a severe credential leak. An attacker can use the compromised SMTP service account credentials to send highly convincing, internal phishing emails (bypassing external spam filters), potentially compromising the mail relay server. The exposed administrator email address also provides a direct target for social engineering.

Remediation:

Immediate Action: Rotate (change) the password for the svcsmtpauthfrant account immediately, as it must be considered compromised.

Restrict access to the Notification page to Super-User accounts only.

Never store or render passwords in cleartext in the backend HTTP response; ensure the server does not send the password to the client at all.

CVSS v4.0 Score: 8.3 (High)
CVSS v4.0 Vector: CVSS:4.0/AV:N/AC:L/AT:P/PR:L/UI:N/VC:H/VI:L/VA:N/SC:L/SI:L/SA:N
